{"test_cases_lookup_map": {"{\"actual_output\": \"Sure, let\\u2019s walk through a concrete, real\\u2011world\\u2011style situation that illustrates how an LLM\\u2011powered system can go very wrong, using only the pieces we covered in the course material.\\n\\nImagine you\\u2019re an AI engineer at a large food\\u2011delivery company\\u2014let\\u2019s call it Zomato. The business gets a huge volume of customer\\u2011service emails every day, and the team builds an LLM\\u2011driven \\u201cauto\\u2011reply\\u201d assistant to draft replies automatically. On paper the pipeline looks solid: a system prompt tells the model how to be polite and helpful; the incoming email is first rewritten into a clean query, then embedded and matched against a vector database of past tickets; a retriever pulls the most relevant past conversations; a reranker orders them; the LLM generates a response; finally an output parser formats the reply and a guardrail checks for prohibited content before the email is sent.\\n\\nNow picture this system in production. Because the offline evaluation looked good, the team pushes it live. Within a few hours a cascade of failures erupts:\\n\\n1. **Component\\u2011level mistake in the system prompt** \\u2013 the prompt that was supposed to tell the model \\u201cnever reveal personal data\\u201d was inadvertently edited, dropping the crucial instruction. As a result, the LLM starts including customers\\u2019 phone numbers in its replies.\\n\\n2. **Retriever and embedding slip\\u2011up** \\u2013 a recent update to the embedding model changes the vector space, so the retriever begins pulling completely unrelated past tickets. The model now thinks a complaint about a late pizza is actually a request for a refund on a subscription, and it generates a mismatched answer.\\n\\n3. **Reranker error** \\u2013 the reranker, which should surface the most relevant past tickets, mis\\u2011ranks the results because its scoring function was mis\\u2011configured. The top\\u2011ranked example is an angry email about a broken dishwasher, completely unrelated to food delivery, leading the LLM to adopt an inappropriate tone.\\n\\n4. **Output parser breakdown** \\u2013 the parser that converts the LLM\\u2019s raw text into the structured email format crashes on a new markdown element the model started using. The system falls back to a raw dump of the model\\u2019s internal token stream, producing a garbled email that looks like gibberish.\\n\\n5. **Guardrail failure** \\u2013 because the guardrail module was disabled during a quick performance tweak, the toxic content from the broken prompt (the personal data leak) slips through unchecked.\\n\\n6. **Production\\u2011time problem of unanticipated inputs** \\u2013 a batch of spam emails containing bizarre Unicode characters reaches the system. The model, never having seen such inputs during offline testing, hallucinates a lengthy, nonsensical apology that includes a link to a malicious site.\\n\\nThe end result is a flood of incorrect, sometimes unsafe emails being sent to customers. Users start receiving replies that contain their private phone numbers, wrong order information, or outright spam. The company\\u2019s reputation takes a hit, support tickets skyrocket, and the auto\\u2011reply system has to be rolled back entirely.\\n\\nWhat this example shows, in line with what we discussed, is that an LLM application can fail badly at many different layers\\u2014prompt design, retrieval, ranking, generation, parsing, and guardrails\\u2014all of which can be exposed to new, unexpected inputs once the system is live. Each of those failure points can cascade, turning a seemingly minor bug into a major production disaster.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"can you give a real-world example of an llm application failing badly?\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.0, "reason": "The score is 0.00 because both the list of reasons for irrelevancy and the list of relevant statements are empty, showing no overlap with the input.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Verdicts:\n[]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"MMLU\\u202fPro was built as a direct upgrade to the original MMLU because researchers saw that the first benchmark had become \\u201csaturated\\u201d: the newest frontier models were all clustering in the high\\u201180s to low\\u201190s, making it hard to tell which model was truly better. To push the evaluation forward, the designers changed a few key ingredients.\\n\\nFirst, they made each multiple\\u2011choice question much harder to answer. In the original MMLU every question had four answer options, so a model only needed to pick the right one out of four. MMLU\\u202fPro expands the choice set to ten options per question, which forces the model to discriminate among many more plausible answers and therefore raises the difficulty level.\\n\\nSecond, they streamlined the subject coverage. The original benchmark spanned 57 subjects, but MMLU\\u202fPro narrowed this down to about twelve subjects. For each of those twelve subjects they kept roughly a thousand questions, ending up with around twelve thousand items in total. This reduction let the creators concentrate on higher\\u2011quality, more challenging items while still preserving breadth across major domains.\\n\\nThird, they added questions that require more reasoning rather than straight factual recall. By inserting \\u201csome such questions which will give some reasoning,\\u201d the new set encourages models to demonstrate deeper understanding instead of merely matching memorized facts.\\n\\nTogether, these changes\\u2014more answer choices, a tighter set of subjects with a consistent thousand\\u2011question per subject load, and the inclusion of reasoning\\u2011heavy items\\u2014make MMLU\\u202fPro a tougher, less saturated benchmark than the original MMLU, giving researchers a more discriminating tool for comparing the latest models.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how did mmlu pro improve on the original mmlu?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"every MCQ had 4 options but in MMLU Pro every question had 10 options so here you have to find the right answer but in 10 given options so you can see that the difficulty level increased after this what they did is they reduced the number of subjects from 57 they came down if I remember correctly they came down to 12 subjects and in 12 subjects they got 12k questions so per subject 1000 questions and they added some such questions\", \"every question had 10 options\", \"they added some such questions which will give some reasoning so overall they made MMLU a little difficult\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 0.8333333333333334, "reason": "The score is 0.83 because the context provides specific improvements like increasing options to 10 and adjusting the subject count, though it is slightly hindered by the vague mention that 'they added some such questions' without further elaboration.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Verdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"they added some such questions which will give some reasoning so overall they made MMLU a little difficult\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"every question had 10 options\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Every MCQ had 4 options but in MMLU Pro every question had 10 options, increasing the difficulty level.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"The number of subjects was reduced from 57 to 12.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"There are 12k questions in total, with 1000 questions per subject.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"They added some such questions.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"no\",\n                \"reason\": \"The phrase 'they added some such questions' is vague and does not provide specific information on how the benchmark was improved.\"\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Sure, let\\u2019s break it down in plain language.  \\n\\nWhen we talk about **drift** in the context of a deployed language\\u2011model system, we\\u2019re really describing a gradual mismatch that shows up over time between the conditions we evaluated the model under (the offline tests) and the reality it faces once it\\u2019s live. Imagine you built a chart\\u2011bot for CampusX, fed it all the current documents, pricing tables, course pages, and then you ran a suite of offline checks\\u2014things like \\u201cdoes the answer stay faithful to the source?\\u201d or \\u201cis the response correct?\\u201d Those checks gave you confidence that the bot works well.  \\n\\nBut once the bot is out in the world, several things can shift:\\n\\n1. **Data drift** \\u2013 the input data the model sees can change even if you haven\\u2019t altered anything yourself. New documents appear, the distribution of user queries evolves, and the model may start seeing patterns it wasn\\u2019t trained on.  \\n\\n2. **Model drift** \\u2013 the model\\u2019s internal behavior can change over time, for example because of updates, fine\\u2011tuning, or subtle bugs that only surface under load.  \\n\\n3. **User drift** \\u2013 the people using the system can change. If, say, a wave of Gen\\u202fZ users replaces a primarily millennial audience, their expectations and phrasing may differ, leading the model to give answers that feel off to them (they might even down\\u2011vote the responses).\\n\\nBecause of any of these drifts, the performance metrics you recorded during offline evaluation start to become **obsolete**. The offline setup assumed a static world; drift means the real\\u2011world world is moving, so the numbers you trusted no longer reflect what\\u2019s actually happening. In practice you\\u2019ll notice the bot\\u2019s faithfulness, relevance, or correctness slipping, latency rising under heavier load, or systematic failures like unexpected bias emerging.  \\n\\nThat\\u2019s why after deployment we keep an eye on drift: we need continuous monitoring (often a \\u201clast\\u201124\\u2011hours\\u201d graph) to catch when the model\\u2019s behavior deviates, so we can intervene\\u2014re\\u2011train, update the knowledge base, or adjust the system\\u2014before the user experience degrades. In short, drift matters because it tells us when our offline testing no longer guarantees good performance in production.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what is drift and why does it matter after deployment?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"And third, drift can occur in the picture.\", \"Drift means that basically, Your offline eval, The testing and deployment you did with the help of which, It starts to get obsolete.\", \"That if the drift starts occurring in some system, Then its eval setup, The offline eval setup, Will become obsolete and its results will not matter.\", \"drift basically means that over time our software performance is not getting bad\", \"model can also drift in fact your user can also drift I am giving a very bad example let's say suddenly millennials used your platform and suddenly gen z's started using so from the expectation point of view there is a drift that what is he expecting, he is not getting the answer he is giving thumbs down\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.6666666666666666, "reason": "The score is 0.67 because while the context provides clear definitions and explains that drift matters because it leads to a 'mismatch between user expectations and model output,' it is partially undermined by irrelevant statements that incorrectly claim drift means 'software performance is not getting bad'.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Verdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"If drift starts occurring in a system, the offline evaluation setup becomes obsolete and its results no longer matter.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"And third, drift can occur in the picture.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"no\",\n                \"reason\": \"The statement only says \\\"drift can occur in the picture\\\" and does not define drift or explain why it matters after deployment.\"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Drift means that basically, your offline eval, the testing and deployment you did with the help of which, it starts to get obsolete.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"drift basically means that over time our software performance is not getting bad\",\n                \"verdict\": \"no\",\n                \"reason\": \"The statement incorrectly defines drift as 'software performance is not getting bad', which is factually inaccurate and does not address why drift matters after deployment.\"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Model drift occurs when there is a change in user demographics or expectations, such as millennials being replaced by Gen Z users.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Drift matters after deployment because it leads to a mismatch between user expectations and model output, resulting in negative feedback like thumbs down.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Vibe testing is the practice of giving an LLM\\u2011powered system just a handful of prompts\\u2014usually five to ten\\u2014just to see whether the answers \\u201cfeel\\u201d right. In other words, you run a few questions that happen to come to mind, look at the responses, and if they seem satisfactory you take that as proof that the system works. The term comes from the idea of \\u201cvibe coding\\u201d: you\\u2019re relying on an informal, subjective impression rather than a systematic check.\\n\\nBecause vibe testing is so casual, it falls far short of what you need before you ship a real product. First, it\\u2019s not repeatable: the exact set of five questions you ask today won\\u2019t be the same set you ask tomorrow, so you can\\u2019t reliably verify that the model behaves consistently over time. Second, it\\u2019s subjective\\u2014what feels correct to one person might hide a subtle error that another would catch. Third, it only scratches the surface of the model\\u2019s behavior; with just a few prompts you\\u2019re unlikely to encounter edge cases, biases, or failure modes that could surface once many users start interacting with the app. As the case studies mentioned, companies that relied on vibe testing and then deployed straight to production ended up with costly mistakes, sometimes even damaging a large brand\\u2019s reputation.\\n\\nIn short, vibe testing works for quick personal experiments, but it doesn\\u2019t provide the thorough, repeatable, and objective evaluation required for production\\u2011grade software. Proper testing\\u2014covering a wide range of inputs, systematic checks, and repeatable procedures\\u2014is essential to avoid the kinds of failures that have plagued real\\u2011world LLM deployments.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what is vibe testing and why isn't it enough for deploying an llm app?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"It is called vibe testing. Just like vibe coding, it is\", \"Vibe testing means you casually trying an LLME application with a few prompts and judging it by feel. You asked 5 questions and read the answers. And judge based on the feel whether it is right or not. This is called vibe testing. So basically what you do is you ask 5-10 questions, the\", \"answers. And judge based on the feel whether it is right or not. This is called vibe testing. So basically what you do is you ask 5-10 questions, the answer is right, you feel that it is working. It is informal, it is subjective, and it is usually not repeatable. Because today you asked 5 questions, after 10 days you won't ask the same 5 questions, you will ask something else. So it is not even repeatable.\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 0.8181818181818182, "reason": "The score is 0.82 because the context provides a clear definition of 'vibe testing' as 'informal, subjective, and usually not repeatable,' though it fails to fully explain why this is insufficient for deployment, as the text cuts off at 'the' and contains incomplete statements like 'Just like vibe coding, it is'.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Verdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Vibe testing means you casually trying an LLME application with a few prompts and judging it by feel.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"You asked 5 questions and read the answers.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"And judge based on the feel whether it is right or not.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"This is called vibe testing.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"So basically what you do is you ask 5-10 questions, the\",\n                \"verdict\": \"no\",\n                \"reason\": \"The statement is incomplete and does not address 'why isn't it enough for deploying an llm app', as it cuts off at 'the'.\"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"It is called vibe testing.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Just like vibe coding, it is\",\n                \"verdict\": \"no\",\n                \"reason\": \"The statement 'Just like vibe coding, it is' is incomplete and does not provide information regarding why vibe testing is insufficient for deploying an LLM app.\"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Vibe testing is judging based on the feel whether an answer is right or not.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"It involves asking 5-10 questions and feeling that the system is working.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Vibe testing is informal, subjective, and usually not repeatable.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Because the set of questions changes over time, the test is not repeatable.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": null\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"When we grade a model\\u2019s output we often need to decide whether the answer it gave is \\u201cthe right one.\\u201d\\u202fIf we relied only on simple string\\u2011matching\\u2014checking whether the generated text is exactly the same sequence of characters as the reference answer\\u2014we would run into a lot of trouble. In practice the correct answer can be expressed in many different ways: it might be capitalized differently, include extra wording, appear as a synonym, or be embedded in a longer sentence (\\u201cthe answer is\\u202fA\\u201d versus just \\u201cA\\u201d). A na\\u00efve exact\\u2011match check would mark all of those as wrong even though they convey the same information.\\n\\nAn LLM used as a judge can read the generated response, understand its meaning, and pull out the core answer (for example, extracting \\u201cA\\u201d from \\u201cthe correct choice is A\\u201d or from \\u201cA is the answer\\u201d). Because the LLM has been trained to comprehend language, it can handle the variety of phrasing, formatting, or surrounding context that simple string matching cannot. The course notes point out that early benchmarks used a version of GPT\\u20114 as a judge for precisely this reason: the judge needed to \\u201cextract A from all kinds of scenarios.\\u201d Later, as newer models became available, they proved even better at extracting the answer correctly, raising the overall accuracy of the evaluation.\\n\\nSo we use an LLM as a judge instead of plain string matching because the LLM can:\\n\\n* Recognize the answer even when it is presented in different forms or embedded in additional text.  \\n* Apply a semantic understanding rather than a brittle character\\u2011by\\u2011character comparison.  \\n\\nThat semantic flexibility is what makes LLM\\u2011based judging more reliable for evaluating model outputs, even though it can still introduce some variability and bias.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"why do we use an llm as a judge instead of simple string matching?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"you use an LLM as a judge because anything can be printed here this can also be the answer is A or it can be printed as A is the answer so you have to extract A from all kinds of scenarios so to do this you use an LLM\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Portkey Multi-Model Gateway (Groq + Gemini)", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}}}